    "chapters": [
      {
        "id": "llama-mlp-precision",
        "title": "Keep Llama gate and up results in FP32 through the SiLU product",
        "claims": [
          {
            "text": "TransformerBlock supplies half-normalized input to the MLP. GatedMLP passes interm_dtype to the gate and up Linear modules for each slice, but passes its separate out_dtype to the down Linear. The activation result goes to the down projection, slice results are summed if needed, and the block adds the MLP result to its residual. Neither the normalization dtype nor that residual interface is edited.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_58019fda08954b92a5adaafcffd33f5e",
              "call_5b327eb2c1214630bf4c89e508ed6d64",
              "call_66f4a3870a6e414bbab06a519e0c21d8"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Linear passes the requested result type to its ordinary-weight and EXL3 backends, including when conversion replaces the former with the latter. With half input, LinearFP16 used the same-dtype torch.matmul branch for the old half result; it now selects hgemm, whose float-output branch requests FP32 compute and output from cuBLAS with two half operands. LinearEXL3 allocates the requested result in both routes: reconstruction uses hgemm and an output transform, while direct EXL3 GEMM passes a float-output flag to its kernel selector. The existing EXL3 rule to reconstruct above 32 flattened rows unless overridden is unchanged.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_6922c997f0f14fecac0066b4cc7ebddd",
              "call_23714e7b39634627b82817999a30fe42",
              "call_02daf8b5ac5b4a05ae3dfdadf18c088c",
              "call_9256c7f2aaee4211908f1e80574479e5",
              "call_800de2e29fa144daa666249b65651920"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Before and after the edit, Llama allocates a separate half destination: the old None setting and the new float setting both differ from torch.half. Only an explicitly half configuration can reuse the up buffer in place. The old SiLU kernel uses half2 arithmetic; the float-input kernel computes g/(1+exp(-g)) times u in float, then narrows and clamps to \u00b165,504, the largest finite binary16 magnitude. This moves the half-storage boundary without promoting the down projection's input.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_58019fda08954b92a5adaafcffd33f5e",
              "call_5b327eb2c1214630bf4c89e508ed6d64",
              "call_33b6a619704a4d7b9ead0f34eb946152"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "For TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0, the pinned config declares LlamaForCausalLM, SiLU, 22 layers, hidden width 2,048 and intermediate width 5,632. This is below the 55,296-channel default slicing threshold and already aligned to the 128-channel padding unit. In all 22 layers the header contains model.layers.<i>.mlp.gate_proj.weight and up_proj.weight as [5632,2048] and down_proj.weight as [2048,5632], all BF16. No MLP bias, inverse scale or EXL3 tensor group is present, so Linear takes the ordinary-weight branch, with neither optional bias nor scaling. The unsliced constructor supplies no alternate or fused MLP key, and there are no extra MLP tensors in this header. The patch adds no tensor lookup and does not derive the runtime result dtype from the checkpoint's BF16 declaration.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_b1c74e7dcd364b43b0676a34b4bd9c0d",
              "call_ad6bad18f0b94d0980e0d6b80e807a83",
              "call_36c45f06102a4064a7cac2edd63a393d",
              "call_5b327eb2c1214630bf4c89e508ed6d64",
              "call_6922c997f0f14fecac0066b4cc7ebddd",
              "call_58019fda08954b92a5adaafcffd33f5e"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "The inspected Mistral-7B-v0.1 and Qwen2.5-0.5B configs also select SiLU and fit a single MLP slice: hidden/intermediate widths are 4,096/14,336 and 896/4,864 respectively. Their subclass constructors inherit the same runtime dtype settings. Unlike the initial architecture entry's historical factory description, the inspected parent configs explicitly supply their respective model subclasses; the change reaches them through their super calls.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_bce8f22ff25d465eb35fdd6126a5d5bb",
              "call_0ca3f54c14bb42a28d3a7cb137bb6804",
              "call_84cb45660be24a7eb65c020fdcdcc272",
              "call_4823308473114af1a83c859792483f55",
              "call_e907cc92edd04f99a4e4c314ec8f82ca",
              "call_36c45f06102a4064a7cac2edd63a393d",
              "call_58019fda08954b92a5adaafcffd33f5e"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "A standard-library check computed gate + up + activation storage per TinyLlama token as 33,792 bytes before and 56,320 bytes after, using two projection buffers and one half destination. It decoded the half finite limit as 65,504 and illustrated saturation with gate=up=400: the idealized product was 160,000, outside finite half range. These are storage arithmetic and a scalar illustration, not GPU measurements.",
            "basis": "execution",
            "evidence": [
              "call_e7fc11ba1cea4170a5c8b6064cec5dc3",
              "call_b1c74e7dcd364b43b0676a34b4bd9c0d",
              "call_5b327eb2c1214630bf4c89e508ed6d64",
              "call_33b6a619704a4d7b9ead0f34eb946152"
            ]
          }
        ]
